.TH YUMED 8 "2026-09-28" "YUME 0.3.0-dev1" "YUME Manual"
.SH NAME
yumed \- native YTP/1 daemon with configured TCP and UDP egress
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B yumed
.B --config
.I path
[
.B --validate
]
.PP
.B yumed
.B --config
.I path
.B --status
.PP
.B yumed
.B --version
.PP
.B yumed
.B --completion
.B bash
.PP
.B yumed
.B --help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yumed
is the native YUME Transport Protocol 1 daemon.
It reads a schema-1 server configuration, serves its configured static
cover site, authenticates clients and carries traffic for declared
services through direct TCP/UDP adapters, module programs or a Linux
managed TUN.
Each service needs a matching adapter; application-owned named services
use the embedding interface.
The daemon checks destination policy before routing and checks every
resolved address before connecting.
.PP
A module adapter's program runs as a supervised child process that
receives its service's streams over a private UNIX socket below
.BR TMPDIR .
The daemon restarts it with backoff when it exits.
See
.BR docs/MODULES.md .
.PP
With a
.B cluster
section the daemon is a member of its operator's cluster and keeps an
authenticated link to every other member it holds secrets for.
The links carry no traffic yet.
.PP
The daemon is not yet qualified for production, and routes over several
servers are not yet available.
Check
.B docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md
before deployment.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--config " path
Read exactly one schema-1 JSON configuration.
Its role must match the program: client for
.BR yume ,
server for
.BR yumed .
Required unless
.B --help
or
.B --version
is requested.
Security policy has no command-line overrides.
.TP
.B --validate
Check schema, role, referenced credentials, direct-adapter destination
policy and the daemon's module programs, then exit without opening a
listener or network connection.
This checks local inputs; it does not test connectivity or complete
runtime startup, including loading the cover site, matching services to
runtime adapters, TUN privileges or DNS authorization.
.TP
.B --version
Print the product version, transport version, configuration schema,
suite, session-security provider, crypto backend and evidence profile.
This operation uses local information and does not read a configuration
or contact a server.
.TP
.BI "--completion " shell
Print the completion script for
.IR shell ,
which must be
.BR bash ,
and exit.
The script completes this program's options, their fixed values and file
arguments.
To load it for one user, save it under the program's name in
.IR ~/\:.local/\:share/\:bash-completion/\:completions/ .
This operation reads no configuration.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the supported options and exit.
Unknown arguments still fail parsing when combined with
.BR --help ,
.B --version
or
.BR --completion .
.PP
The status option asks the running daemon of the same configuration for
its state.
.TP
.B --status
Connect to the control socket that
.B control.socket
names, print the running daemon's state and exit.
The report names the listeners, the number of client sessions and, for a
cluster member, the list's serial and expiry and each peer's links: the
outbound link's state, how long it has lasted or when the next attempt
starts and its last failure, and the peer's inbound sessions.
The exit status is 2 when the configuration names no control socket and
1 when no daemon answers on it.
.B --status
takes no option besides
.BR --config .
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration owns endpoint addresses, exact suite, credential file
references, cover profile, services, adapters and resource limits, and
optionally a control socket and a server's cluster membership.
The schema rejects unknown fields, wrong types and unsupported values.
Credential paths are relative to the configuration file's directory
unless absolute.
Credential loading rejects unsafe file types and permissions; inline
secrets are forbidden.
.SH CLUSTER
A server's
.B cluster
object names five files:
.BR operator_key ,
the operator's composite public key,
.BR list ,
the cluster list,
.BR signature ,
its composite signature,
.BR peers ,
the peer store, and
.BR state ,
where the daemon saves the highest list serial it has loaded.
The list names at most 64 servers with their addresses and public keys,
and the operator signs it with a key that stays off the servers.
The daemon refuses to start with a list whose signature does not verify,
whose
.B not_after
has passed or that does not name the daemon itself.
The peer store gives each peer an outbound PSK, an inbound PSK and the
peer's admission key.
Every PSK differs from every other PSK, admission key and client PSK,
and a peer is never also a client or administrator.
All five files are owner-only like the other credential files.
The daemon creates and replaces
.B state
itself, so its directory must exist and be writable by the daemon, for
example
.B /var/lib/yume/cluster-state.json
under the packaged unit, whose
.B /etc/yume
is read-only.
It refuses a list with a lower serial than the saved one, after a
restart too, and a state file of another cluster.
.B yume-setup
writes all of it:
.BR cluster-init ,
.BR cluster-add ,
.B cluster-remove
and
.BR cluster-sign .
The format is in
.BR docs/protocol/CLUSTER_1.md .
.PP
Once its listeners accept, the daemon keeps one outbound link to each
peer, a YTP/1 session like a client's that offers no service, and
reconnects with backoff when it ends.
Each peer's own link arrives like a client's session: the peer may hold
two at once and may open no stream.
When the list's
.B not_after
passes, the daemon closes its links and ends the peers' sessions until a
reload loads a newer list.
.SH COVER AND ADMISSION
The daemon loads an operator-provided static site with explicit index
and not-found files.
It has no built-in fallback site or reverse-proxy backend.
Ordinary cover requests can use HTTP/1.1 or HTTP/2 over TLS 1.2 or TLS
1.3.
YTP/1 promotion requires TLS 1.3, HTTP/2 and authenticated admission
bound to the connection's TLS exporter.
Failed admission remains cover traffic.
.SH PRIVILEGE
Run as an unprivileged user.
Prefer a high listen port or grant only the low-port binding capability
needed for port 443.
The native daemon does not implement automatic privilege dropping or
host firewall configuration.
Managed TUN requires
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
and access to
.B /dev/net/tun
in its network namespace; configured per-link DNS also requires
systemd-resolved authorization.
Module programs run as the daemon's user without capabilities.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
stop the daemon after its sessions close, followed by removal of owned
TUN networking.
Modules receive
.BR SIGTERM ,
then
.B SIGKILL
after five seconds.
.B SIGHUP
reloads the authorized and admin key stores and the server's composite
and ML-KEM keys without dropping other clients.
Every later session authenticates against them, including one on a
connection accepted before the reload.
Sessions of removed identities, or beyond a lowered per-identity
.BR max_sessions ,
end.
Established sessions apply changed grants to their next stream and a
changed
.B weight
to their next transfer.
A cluster member also reads its list, routes view and peer store again,
and serves the new view on
.BR yume.routes .
A link whose peer and inputs are unchanged keeps running, a changed
peer's link is replaced, a removed peer's link closes and a new peer
gets one.
A newer serial is saved before anything switches.
An invalid store, a list naming another operator, a list with a lower
serial or a state file that cannot be written is refused and the
previous credentials stay in force.
TLS material, the admission key and egress lists are read only at
startup, and a changed admission key is refused.
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
With
.B control.socket
set, the running program listens on that UNIX socket for processes of
its own user.
The directory must belong to that user and be closed to writes by group
and others, the socket is mode 0600, and a connection from another user
is closed.
At most four connections are open at once.
.PP
A connection sends one line, the JSON object
.BR {"control":1,"request":"status"} ,
within two seconds and in at most 512 bytes.
The program answers with one JSON object on one line and closes the
connection, and
.B --status
refuses an answer over 64 KiB.
Any other request gets an object that holds
.B control
and an
.B error
text.
No answer holds a key, credential or payload.
.PP
The daemon's answer holds
.B control
(1),
.BR program ,
.BR version ,
.BR listeners ,
.B client_sessions
and
.BR cluster ,
null outside a cluster.
A member's
.B cluster
holds
.BR id ,
.BR serial ,
.BR not_after ,
.BR expired ,
.BR self ,
.B circuits
and
.BR links ,
one per peer with
.BR peer ,
.BR identity ,
.BR outbound ,
.B inbound
and
.BR circuits .
.B outbound
holds
.BR state ,
.BR sessions ,
.BR failed_attempts ,
.B last_failure
and, as for a client,
.B connected_ms
or
.BR retry_ms .
.B inbound
holds
.B sessions
and, while there is one,
.B connected_ms
of the oldest.
A link's
.B circuits
holds
.BR in ,
the circuits that arrive over the peer's link, and
.BR out ,
those this server extended over its link to the peer.
The cluster's
.B circuits
holds
.BR exit ,
whether this server is an exit,
.BR open ,
.BR entry ,
.BR relayed ,
.BR exit_streams ,
.B failed
and
.BR refused ,
which counts refusals since start as
.BR total ,
.BR client_circuits ,
.BR circuit_rate ,
.B handshakes
and
.BR streams .
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates successful help, version or validation output, or
shutdown requested by a handled signal with successful cleanup.
Exit status 1 indicates a runtime or internal failure.
Exit status 2 indicates invalid arguments, configuration or startup
prerequisites.
Diagnostics go to standard error; help and version output go to standard
output.
Argument errors also print usage on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate an existing server kit, then start its configured listeners:
.PP
.nf
yumed --config /path/to/server.json --validate
yumed --config /path/to/server.json
.fi
.PP
The separately installed
.B yume-setup
and
.B yume-doctor
tools provision and inspect schema-1 kits.
Their
.B --help
output describes their arguments.
